    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've been having a major issue with my dark chocolate batches lately. Even when I follow my usual process, I'm getting this weird white, dull coating on the surface after it sets—like a fat bloom. Can you explain the science behind why this happens and what I'm doing wrong with my tempering?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white coating on dark chocolate is called blooming or fat blooming, which occurs when cocoa butter migrates to the surface of the chocolate as it's exposed to heat, humidity, or temperature fluctuations. This migration can be caused by incomplete tempering, where the chocolate doesn't reach the optimal crystal structure. In tempering, you're aiming to create a stable crystal structure that prevents the cocoa butter from migrating; if your chocolate isn't tempered correctly, the crystals can be unstable, leading to blooming.",
